{# table; title: Fey.nvim orgmode conversion task list; kind: tasks #}
{# labels, tasks, roadmap, orgmode-port #}

  I. About this list

Fey.nvim started as a fork of nvim-orgmode/orgmode. The first features (headings,
lists, tables, tags, the vault, queries, databases) are already native Fey. This
list covers everything that is still orgmode code written against the org
grammar (tree-sitter-org) and therefore does not work, or only half works, on
the fey grammar.

The rule for every task below: reuse what can be reused (the pure logic in
`objects/date.lua`, `objects/duration.lua`, `objects/calendar.lua`, the agenda
view/token/sorting/filter code, the capture window, the ui menus) and replace
only the part that reads or writes org nodes.

  I.A. How to read a task

Every task has the same fields.

-  Now:  what is broken today and where it lives.
-  Reuse:  what survives unchanged.
-  Fey:  how the feature is spelled in Fey. Either a tag (name, parameters,
   handler) or a grammar rule (and scanner work, if any). A task never needs
   both unless it says so.
-  Triggers:  autocmds and keybinds that start it.
-  Vault:  whether the vault index (`.fey/vault.db`, `fey.vault`, `fey.query`)
   should be used, and how that improves the feature.
-  After:  tasks that must be finished first.


  I.B. State of the code today

What the fey grammar gives us: `document`, `section`, `heading` (a `signature`
and a `title`, nothing else), `body`, `paragraph`, `list`/`listitem`/`bullet`,
`table`, `block` (fenced), and four tag formats: `scope_tag`, `line_tag`,
`block_tag` and `pair_tag`. A heading title may contain tags, and any body may
contain them.

What it does not have, and the org code still assumes: `item`, `tags`, `plan`,
`property_drawer`, `timestamp`, `entry`, `drawer`, `checkbox`, `directive`,
`fndef`, `comment`, `dynamic_block`, `latex_env`.

What already works and the tasks build on:

-  the tag handler registry in `files/elements/tags` (`Tag.handlers[name][node_type]`,
   `Tag.parse_tag_node`, `Tag.parse_all_tags`) with the handlers `nvim`, `hl`,
   `query`, `feydb`, `link`, `section`
-  the vault: tables `files`, `headings`, `tags`, `links`, `labels`, `properties`,
   incremental indexing, the `FeyVaultFileIndexed` event, `Vault:index_path`
-  the query language (`fey.query`), which today refuses TASK queries and returns
   empty `tasks`/`lists` fields because there is no task index
-  heading and list reindexing, promote/demote, subtree moves, tables, folds,
   indent, text objects, edit special on fenced blocks

  I.B.i. The key finding

The fey heading has no slots. In org a headline carried a todo keyword, a
priority, a tag list, a planning line and a property drawer. In Fey all of that
has to be tags, and the tags already exist as a mechanism: the vault records
every tag with its `heading_ord`, so a tag in a heading title or in the first
lines of its body belongs to that heading.

That means almost every conversion below is a new tag name plus a handler plus
index support, not new grammar. Only the checkbox needs a grammar rule. One
optional rule (`metadata`) and one rejected alternative (an inline `timestamp`
node) are described so the decision is recorded.

  I.B.ii. Conventions proposed for the new tags

-  metadata tags use the `#` sigil in scope format. Reference-like inline tags
   (dates, footnote references) use the `@` sigil, like `link` and `section`.
   Handlers only look at the tag name and node type, so this is a convention
   and not a rule.
-  every new tag name is a config option `fey_<x>_tag_name`, like the existing
   `fey_hl_tag_name`, so a user can rename it.
-  a tag handler lives in `lua/fey/files/elements/tags/handlers/<name>.lua`,
   exports `handlers = { scope_tag = f, line_tag = f, block_tag = f, pair_tag = f }`
   and is registered in `Tag.setup`.
-  all example syntax in this file is in fenced blocks, so it stays raw text.


  I.C. Naming and documentation rules while the conversion runs

Two rules apply to every task below, so that the clean up at the end stays
small.

-  Naming:  new code, new options and new text use Fey words from the start
   (the table in III.R). Where an old org name is in the way, the new code
   still calls it by its Fey name in comments and docs, and the rename itself
   waits for III.R so a finished area is touched once. For example the heading
   method `get_tags` returns labels: say labels everywhere new.
-  Documentation:  progressive, in three steps.
   1. Each task, when it is finished, gets a short user facing section in
      `README.fey` (feature set, keys, an example) and its Done entry in this
      file says which docs it touched.
   2. At the end of each phase a checkpoint task goes over everything the phase
      changed (II.R for phase 1), including the vault tutorial of the test
      vault and the generated `doc/fey_api.txt`.
   3. The complete rewrite of the old org docs and the help files is III.S, at
      the very end.


  II. Phase 1: timestamps, agenda, refile

These come first. The order inside the phase is the order to build them: the
prerequisites (A to H) unblock the agenda (I to L), then refile (M to O).

  II.A. Tag toolkit and the heading metadata region

-  Now:  `Tag.parse_all_tags` returns `false` when the tree has any error, and
   there is no way to find the tag at the cursor, to find the tags that belong
   to one heading, or to rewrite one tag in place. `FeyApi.link_text` is the only
   tag writer and it is link specific.
-  Reuse:  `Tag.parse_tag_node`, the pure tag parser in `vault/extract.lua`,
   `head_text` escaping in `api/init.lua`, the `fey_tags` query.
-  Fey:  Lua only first. A new module `fey.files.elements.tags.edit` with
   `at_cursor(bufnr)`, `for_heading(heading)`, `build(name, values, key_values,
   opts)` (escapes `\`, `,`, `;`), `replace(tag, new_text)` and `set_key(tag,
   key, value)` that keeps the rest of the head untouched.
   Define the metadata region of a heading as: the tags in its title, plus the
   leading lines of its body that hold only tags. This replaces the org `plan`
   and `property_drawer` fields.
-  Fey:  optional grammar rule, decide after the Lua version is in use: give
   `section` a `metadata` field (the rule is already commented out in
   `grammar.js`) made of `_standalone_scope_tag` lines and tag-only paragraphs.
   The difficulty is the conflict with `paragraph`: both start with a tag. It
   needs a declared conflict and `prec.dynamic`, and probably no scanner work.
   Only do it if the Lua helper is too slow or too ambiguous.
-  Triggers:  none, it is infrastructure.
-  Vault:  the extractor must strip the metadata tags from `headings.title`
   (and from the heading `path`), otherwise queries and section links see
   `Write report {# todo, TODO #}` as the title. Check this first.
-  After:  nothing.

Done (2026-10-06):

-  Chose:  the Lua only version. The `metadata` grammar rule is still open and
   is to be decided after phase 1, once the helpers have been used.
-  Module:  `lua/fey/files/elements/tags/edit.lua`. Reading: `at_cursor`
   (innermost tag around the cursor, works in a buffer with errors elsewhere;
   option `line` falls back to the first tag at or after the cursor on the
   line) and `for_heading` (the metadata region, see below).
   Writing: `build`, `set_key`, `set_value`, `remove_value`, `rename`, `remove`,
   `add_to_title` and `add_to_region`.
-  Region:  the tags in the title of a heading, plus the leading body lines
   that hold nothing but tags. Blank lines between them are fine. A paragraph
   spans lines, so a tag that shares its line with text, or a line tag that has
   text after its head, is content and ends the region. `for_heading` returns
   the tags with `region` (`title` or `body`) and `row`, and the last body row
   of the region.
-  Edits:  targeted. `set_key` replaces only the value of an existing key, or
   adds `; key: value` at the end of the head; `nil` removes the key. A
   multi-line head keeps its layout. Values are escaped (`\`, `,`, `;`) and
   refused when they could not be read back: a line break, a leading closing
   bracket, or a blank followed by the end of the tag. Tags read before an
   edit are stale afterwards, read them again.
-  Placement:  `add_to_title` puts a tag at the end of the title, or in front
   of the first word with `first` (where a todo keyword goes). `add_to_region`
   adds a line after the last tag line of the region, or directly under the
   heading when it has none, indented like the body of the heading.
-  Vault:  done. The extractor leaves the tags named in the new option
   `vault.meta_tags` out of `headings.title` and `headings.path`, and out of
   the keys of data headings. Default: label, labels, todo, priority, prop,
   scheduled, deadline, closed. Only scope and line tags in the title are
   dropped, the blanks after them go with them, and a title without such a tag
   is untouched. Other tags (a link in a title, for example) stay in the title.
   Vault files already indexed keep their old titles until they change on disk,
   or a full reindex (`<prefix>vr` with a full scan).
-  Tests:  `tests/tags_edit.lua` (45 checks).

Example:

    ###  src lua
    local edit = require('fey.files.elements.tags.edit')

    -- the tags that belong to the heading at the cursor
    local tags, last_row = edit.for_heading(bufnr, heading_node, { name = 'prop' })

    -- change a key of the tag under the cursor, or remove it with nil
    local tag = edit.at_cursor(bufnr, { line = true, name = 'prop' })
    edit.set_key(tag, 'effort', '3h')

    -- add tags to a heading
    edit.add_to_title(bufnr, heading_node, edit.build('todo', { 'TODO' }), { first = true })
    edit.add_to_region(bufnr, heading_node, edit.build('scheduled', { '2026-10-06 Tue' }))
    ###

  II.B. Date tag and the FeyDate object

-  Now:  `FeyDate.from_node` and `from_fey_date` expect the text of an org
   `timestamp` node wrapped in angle or square brackets. Fey has no such node,
   and a bare angle-bracket date is just plain text.
-  Reuse:  almost all of `objects/date.lua` (1098 lines: arithmetic, spans,
   repeaters, humanize, adjustments), `duration.lua`, `calendar.lua`. Only
   the node-facing constructors change. `FeyDate.from_string` already parses
   `2026-10-06 Tue 10:00 +1w -3d`, so the tag value can be that text unchanged.
-  Fey:  tag `date` (config `fey_date_tag_name`), scope or line format.
   Head value: the inside text of an org timestamp. A range is `a--b`.
   Key-values: `active: false` for an inactive date (default active), and
   optional structured overrides `repeat`, `warn`, `time`.
   Handler `date`: opening the tag at point opens the calendar picker (a change
   on write), never an automatic run.
   New constructor `FeyDate.from_tag(tag)`, plus `FeyDate:to_tag_text()` that
   writes it back through the tag toolkit (II.A). Remove the `from_node`
   logbook special case (`is_date_in_drawer`).
-  Fey:  alternative, rejected for now: a grammar `timestamp` node
   (`<...>` active, `[...]` inactive) with fields `date`, `day`, `time`,
   `duration`, `repeat`, `delay`, as in tree-sitter-org. It would need a
   zero-width external gate `_timestamp_start`, emitted by the scanner after it
   checks for digits and dashes after the bracket, the same pattern as
   `_scope_tag_start`, because `<` and `[` are ordinary tokens and also open
   tags, pair tags and checkboxes. The cost is high and the tag does the job.
-  Triggers:  insert mappings below (II.D). Update the insert abbreviations in
   `ftplugin/fey.lua` (`:today:`, `:now:`, `:itoday:`, `:inow:`) to expand to
   the tag text instead of the angle bracket text.
-  Vault:  yes. The extractor records every date in a new `dates` table
   (see II.I), with epoch start and end, a time flag, active flag, repeater,
   warning delay, the tag kind and the owning `heading_ord`. Agenda, overdue
   queries, calendars and the query language all read it, so nothing re-parses
   text.
-  After:  II.A.

Done (2026-10-06):

-  Chose:  the tag, as proposed. The grammar `timestamp` node stays rejected.
-  Tag:  `date`, name from `fey_date_tag_name` (default `date`). The first
   value is the timestamp text exactly as it was inside an org timestamp. The
   keys: `active: false` for an inactive date, `time`, `repeat`, `warn` (`warn:
   3d` means the delay `-3d`) that add to the timestamp, and a range is
   `start--end`. The planning tags get their names now too:
   `fey_scheduled_tag_name`, `fey_deadline_tag_name`, `fey_closed_tag_name`,
   and they read the same way. A closed date is inactive unless it says
   `active: true`; every other kind is active unless it says `active: false`.
-  FeyDate reading:  `FeyDate.from_tag_value(text, opts)` (also what the vault
   will use, it needs no buffer), `FeyDate.from_tag(tag, opts)` and
   `FeyDate.from_node(node, source, opts)`, which now takes a tag node and a
   buffer number or the source text. The type (`SCHEDULED`, `DEADLINE`,
   `CLOSED`, `NONE`) comes from the tag name. The range of a date is the range
   of its text in the buffer, split for the two ends of a range.
-  FeyDate writing:  `FeyDate:to_tag_value()` and `FeyDate:to_tag_text(opts)`,
   which writes `{@ date, ... @}`, or `{# scheduled, ... #}` and its siblings
   for a planning date. `to_wrapped_string` is still there, because the
   capture templates, logbook, todo and note code still call it; each of those
   tasks moves to the tag text as it is ported.
-  Handler:  `lua/fey/files/elements/tags/handlers/date.lua`, registered for
   all four names. Applying it opens the calendar on the date and writes the
   chosen day back with `edit.set_value`. A range moves its start and keeps its
   end, a repeater and a warning delay stay. The tag is looked up again after
   the calendar closes, because the calendar is asynchronous.
-  Trigger:  `fey_open_at_point` (`<prefix>o`) on a date tag opens the calendar
   (it tried links only before). The insert abbreviations `:today:`, `:now:`,
   `:itoday:` and `:inow:` now expand to the tag text. The mappings that edit a
   date under the cursor are II.D.
-  Tests:  `tests/date_tag.lua` (37 checks).

Example:

    ###  src fey
    {@ date, 2026-10-06 Tue 10:00-11:00 +1w -3d @}
    {@ date, 2026-10-06 Tue--2026-10-08 Thu; active: false @}
    {# deadline, 2026-10-10; warn: 3d; time: 12:30 #}
    {# closed, 2026-10-09 Fri #}
    ###

Example:

    ###  src fey
    {@ date, 2026-10-06 Tue 10:00-11:00 +1w -3d @}
    {@ date, 2026-10-06--2026-10-08; active: false @}
    ###


  II.C. Planning dates: scheduled, deadline, closed

-  Now:  `Heading:get_plan_dates`, `set_scheduled_date`, `set_deadline_date`,
   `set_closed_date`, `_add_date`, `_remove_date`, `get_append_line` read the
   org `plan` field and entries. The mappings `fey_deadline`, `fey_schedule`
   exist but are commented out.
-  Reuse:  the `FeyDate` type sorting (`is_deadline`, `is_scheduled`,
   `get_type_sort_value`), the menus, `Heading:get_valid_dates_for_agenda`.
-  Fey:  tags `scheduled`, `deadline`, `closed` (config
   `fey_scheduled_tag_name` and so on), scope format, same head value as the
   `date` tag, in the metadata region of the heading (II.A). Handlers:
   `scope_tag` only, applied from the insert mappings. Heading API
   keeps its method names, now backed by tags: they read the metadata region and
   write through the tag toolkit.
-  Triggers:  keybinds `fey_deadline` `<prefix>id`, `fey_schedule` `<prefix>is`,
   `fey_time_stamp` `<prefix>i.`, `fey_time_stamp_inactive` `<prefix>i!`.
   Autocmd: none. `closed` is written by the todo task (II.E).
-  Vault:  yes. `dates.kind` holds the three kinds, so "what is overdue" is one
   SQL statement. A heading can also be sorted by deadline in a database view.
-  After:  II.A, II.B.

Done (2026-10-06):

-  Chose:  the tags `scheduled`, `deadline` and `closed`, scope format, with
   the `#` sigil, one value each (the timestamp text of the `date` tag).
   Names from `fey_scheduled_tag_name`, `fey_deadline_tag_name` and
   `fey_closed_tag_name`. They live in the metadata region of the heading.
-  Heading API:  the method names are the same, now backed by tags.
   `get_plan_dates` (dates by kind, the tag nodes, whether there are any),
   `get_scheduled_date`, `get_deadline_date`, `get_closed_date`,
   `get_non_plan_dates` (the `date` tags in the title and the body of the
   heading, not its subsections), `get_all_dates`,
   `set_scheduled_date`, `set_deadline_date`, `set_closed_date` and the
   `remove_*_date` methods. `get_append_line` is the line after the last tag
   line of the region, or the line under the heading.
-  Writing:  a date of a kind that is already there is replaced in place. A new
   one goes next to the other planning tags on their line (in the title or in
   the body), or onto a line of its own directly under the heading. Removing
   the only tag on a line removes the line. `closed` is written inactive,
   which is its default and needs no key.
-  Triggers:  the mappings `fey_deadline` (`<prefix>id`), `fey_schedule`
   (`<prefix>is`), `fey_time_stamp` (`<prefix>i.`) and `fey_time_stamp_inactive`
   (`<prefix>i!`) are enabled. The first two open the calendar with a clear
   key, and setting one removes the `closed` tag, as before. The last two
   change the date under the cursor, or insert a date tag after the cursor.
-  Vault:  the index of the planning tags is the `dates` table of II.I, not
   done yet.
-  Tests:  `tests/heading_dates.lua` (35 checks, with II.D).

Example:

    ###  src fey
      I.A. Write the report {# deadline, 2026-10-10 Sat -3d #}
    {# scheduled, 2026-10-06 Tue 10:00 #}

    A meeting on {@ date, 2026-10-08 Thu 14:00 @}.
    ###


  II.D. Timestamp editing and the calendar

-  Now:  `timestamp_up/down(_day)`, `change_date`, `fey_toggle_timestamp_type`,
   `_get_date_under_cursor`, `_replace_date`, `_adjust_date` work on the org
   node under the cursor. The calendar popup (`objects/calendar.lua`) calls
   them. All mappings are commented out.
-  Reuse:  the calendar window and the month arithmetic, `_adjust_date_part`
   (it works on text columns inside the date and only needs the new range),
   the `fey_time_stamp_rounding_minutes` option.
-  Fey:  tag `date` and its relatives (II.B, II.C). The cursor lookup is the
   tag toolkit `at_cursor`. A part (year, month, day, hour, minute) is chosen by
   the column inside the head value. `fey_toggle_timestamp_type` flips the
   `active` key-value.
-  Triggers:  keybinds `<S-UP>` and `<S-DOWN>` (day), `<C-a>` and `<C-x>`
   (the part under the cursor), `cid` (change with the calendar picker),
   `<prefix>d!` (toggle active). Insert mode abbreviations as in II.B.
-  Vault:  small. Drop the date picker's own cache and use the vault to offer
   "dates already used in this vault" as picker suggestions. No index change.
-  After:  II.B.

Done (2026-10-06):

-  Chose:  as proposed, on the tag toolkit. The date under the cursor is the
   date of the date or planning tag around the cursor (`edit.at_cursor`). It
   works anywhere in the tag, in a table cell too. In a range the end date counts
   from the first character of the end on.
-  Writing back:  `FeyMappings:_replace_date` replaces the text of that date in
   place (the range of a `FeyDate` is the range of its text) and keeps the
   `active` key of the tag in step with the date: the key is written when the
   date differs from the default of its kind and dropped when it does not.
-  Mappings enabled:  `fey_timestamp_up_day` (`<S-UP>`) and
   `fey_timestamp_down_day` (`<S-DOWN>`), which adjust anywhere in the tag;
   `fey_timestamp_up` (`<C-a>`) and `fey_timestamp_down` (`<C-x>`), which
   adjust the part under the cursor (year, month, day, hour, minutes, the
   repeater) and fall through to the normal key when there is no date there;
   `fey_change_date` (`cid`) and `fey_toggle_timestamp_type` (`<prefix>d!`),
   which flips the `active` key. The part adjustment no longer toggles active
   on the brackets, the tag has no brackets around the text.
-  Calendar:  unchanged. The open-at-point handler of II.B and `cid` both use
   it.
-  Vault:  not used, as proposed. The suggestion of dates already used in the
   vault waits for the `dates` table of II.I.
-  Tests:  `tests/heading_dates.lua`.

  II.E. Todo state

-  Now:  `Heading:get_todo`, `set_todo`, `update_todo_cookie`,
   `FeyMappings:_todo_change_state`, `objects/todo_state.lua`,
   `todo_keywords/` work on the first org `item` child. The events
   `TodoChanged` and the listeners `AlignTags` and `ClockOut` hang off it.
-  Reuse:  `todo_keywords` (keyword list, `|` split, fast access, faces),
   `todo_state` (next/prev logic, repeat to state), the events and listeners,
   the face highlighting in `colors/highlighter/todos.lua`.
-  Fey:  tag `todo` (config `fey_todo_tag_name`) with one value, the keyword.
   It must be the first tag of the heading title, the same rule org had for the
   keyword. Handler `scope_tag`: cycle to the next state.
   Optionally a key-value `at: <date>` to remember when it changed, instead of
   a `closed` tag for users that do not want one.
   Because the raw tag is noisy, add concealment: a highlights query that
   conceals the tag syntax in heading titles and shows only the keyword with
   its face. Controlled by a new option `fey_conceal_task_tags`, off by
   default, next to `fey_hide_emphasis_markers`.
-  Triggers:  keybinds `fey_todo` `cit`, `fey_todo_prev` `ciT`,
   `fey_insert_todo_heading` `<prefix>iT`, `fey_insert_todo_heading_respect_content`
   `<prefix>it`. Autocmd: none, the events are dispatched by the mapping.
   Writing `DONE` also writes the `closed` tag (II.C, `fey_log_done`).
-  Vault:  yes. The extractor writes `tasks.state` and `tasks.done` (II.I) so
   the agenda and a TASK query can list open work across files without
   opening any buffer.
-  After:  II.A, II.C.

Done (2026-10-06):

-  Chose:  the tag `todo` (name from `fey_todo_tag_name`), one value, the
   keyword, and it has to be the first thing of the heading title. A todo tag
   anywhere else in the title is just a tag, not a keyword. The keyword list
   is the config one, or the `todo` key of the document data
   (`{# table; todo: TODO NEXT | DONE #}`), which replaces the org `#+todo`.
-  Heading API:  `get_todo` (keyword, tag node, type, index), `is_todo`,
   `is_done`, `set_todo(keyword)` (replaces the value, adds the tag first in the
   title, or removes it with an empty keyword), and `get_title`, which now
   leaves the metadata tags (`vault.meta_tags`) out of the title text.
-  Concealment:  default ON. `colors/highlighter/task_tags.lua` hides every part
   of the todo, priority and labels tags of a title except their values: the
   head `{# todo, ` and the end ` #}` (and any keys) are concealed, so only the
   keyword shows, with its face (`@fey.keyword.todo`, `@fey.keyword.done`, or
   the face of the keyword from `fey_todo_keyword_faces`). The cursor line
   shows the tags as written (the default 'concealcursor'), so editing is not
   blind. Windows get 'conceallevel' 2, like for the emphasis markers.
-  Switches:  the option `fey_conceal_task_tags` (default true), the buffer
   variable `b:fey_conceal_task_tags` (set by the toggle, wins over the
   option) and the keybind `fey_toggle_conceal_task_tags`, default
   `<prefix>Tc`. See III.P about a toggle tree.
-  Triggers:  `fey_todo` (`cit`) and `fey_todo_prev` (`ciT`) are enabled, the
   events `TodoChanged` still go out, and `closed` is written and removed
   through II.C (`fey_log_done`). Open-at-point (`<prefix>o`) on the keyword
   cycles it: handler `todo.lua`, and the registry `Tag.at_point` lists the
   tags that the open-at-point mapping applies the handler of (todo, priority
   and the date family).
-  Not done:  the optional `at:` key. The notes written on a state change
   (closing note, repeat note) and the repeat bookkeeping still use the org
   text and the org drawer until III.E, and `ClockOut` waits for III.A.
   The vault side (`tasks.state`) is II.I.
-  Tests:  `tests/heading_task.lua` (71 checks, with II.F to II.H).

Changed (2026-10-06): the `todo` and `priority` tags above became one tag,
`status`, by decision. This replaces what is written above for II.E and II.F;
the text stays for the history.

-  Tag:  `status` (`fey_status_tag_name`, default `status`). Its first plain
   value is the todo keyword, its second plain value is the priority:
   `{# status, TODO, A #}`. Without a keyword the priority is written with the
   key: `{# status; priority: A #}`. A tag without either is removed. The tag
   is still the first thing of the title.
-  Heading API:  the same methods (`get_todo`, `set_todo`, `get_priority`,
   `set_priority`, `get_title`). Every write goes through one writer that
   rebuilds the tag from the keyword and the priority, so setting one keeps
   the other, the tag moves between the two forms by itself (removing the
   keyword turns `status, TODO, A` into `status; priority: A`, setting one
   turns it back), and other keys of the tag are kept. `set_todo('')` and
   `set_priority('')` remove just their part.
-  Display:  one tag, two values: the head and the end are hidden and the comma
   between the values with them, so `{# status, TODO, A #}` shows as `TODO A`
   with the keyword face and the priority face. The key form shows just the
   priority.
-  Config and registry:  `fey_todo_tag_name` and `fey_priority_tag_name` are
   gone (nothing outside this session used them), `vault.meta_tags` has
   `status` in place of `todo` and `priority`, the handler is
   `handlers/status.lua` and `Tag.at_point` lists `status`.
   Open-at-point cycles the todo state, or raises the priority when the tag
   has no keyword.
-  Mappings:  unchanged (`cit`, `ciT`, `<prefix>,`, `ciR`, `cir`).
-  Vault:  II.I reads `tasks.state` from the first value and `tasks.priority`
   from the second value or the key, of the `status` tag.
-  Tests:  `tests/heading_task.lua` (82 checks).

Example:

    ###  src fey
      I.A. {# todo, TODO #} Write the report
      I.B. {# todo, DONE #} {# priority, A #} Ship it
    ###


  II.F. Priority

-  Now:  `Heading:get_priority`, `set_priority`, `get_priority_sort_value`,
   `objects/priority_state.lua`, the mappings `priority`, `priority_up`,
   `priority_down`. They match the org cookie text.
-  Reuse:  `priority_state` (A/B/C range, default, cycle), sorting values.
-  Fey:  tag `priority` (config `fey_priority_tag_name`), one value, directly
   after the `todo` tag in the title. A priority without a todo tag is allowed.
   Optional merged form `{# todo, TODO; priority: A #}` is parsed but not
   written.
-  Triggers:  keybinds `fey_priority` `<prefix>,`, `fey_priority_up` `ciR`,
   `fey_priority_down` `cir`.
-  Vault:  yes, `tasks.priority`. Sorting by priority in agenda and queries.
-  After:  II.A.

Done (2026-10-06):

-  Chose:  the tag `priority` (`fey_priority_tag_name`), one value, in the
   title. Only a value that is a priority of the config counts. A priority
   without a todo tag is fine.
-  Heading API:  `get_priority`, `set_priority` (replaces the value, removes the
   tag with an empty one; a new tag goes right after the todo tag, or first),
   `get_priority_sort_value`. The merged form `{# todo, TODO; priority: A #}`
   from the proposal was not done: one tag, one thing.
-  Triggers:  `fey_priority` (`<prefix>,`), `fey_priority_up` (`ciR`) and
   `fey_priority_down` (`cir`) are enabled. Open-at-point on the value raises
   the priority one step (handler `priority.lua`).
-  Display:  concealed like the todo tag (II.E), the value takes the face of
   its priority (`@fey.priority.highest`, `.high`, `.default`, ...).
-  Vault:  `tasks.priority` is II.I.

Changed (2026-10-06): there is no `priority` tag any more, the priority is the
second value of the `status` tag, or its `priority` key without a keyword.
See the Changed entry of II.E for the format, the writer and the display.
`fey_priority_tag_name` is gone.

  II.G. Heading tags (org tag lists) become labels

-  Now:  `Heading:get_tags`, `set_tags`, `add_tag`, `remove_tag`, `toggle_tag`,
   `get_own_tags`, `tags_to_string`, `align_tags`, and the `AlignTags` event
   listener read an org `tags` field and align it at `fey_tags_column`.
-  Reuse:  tag inheritance logic (`fey_use_tag_inheritance`,
   `fey_tags_exclude_from_inheritance`), `config:exclude_tags`,
   the tag-string utilities.
-  Fey:  the existing `labels` tag (config `vault.label_tags`). Terminology
   stays as the vault has it: a Fey tag is syntax, topical categories are
   labels. `get_tags` returns labels. `set_tags` rewrites the `labels` tag in the
   heading title. `align_tags` becomes display only: a virtual text extmark
   at the end of the heading line that shows the labels, right aligned at
   `fey_tags_column`, drawn on `BufferChanged` (so the raw tag may be
   concealed, see II.E).
-  Triggers:  keybinds `fey_set_tags_command` `<prefix>t`,
   `fey_toggle_archive_tag` `<prefix>A`. Autocmd: the existing reindex
   events redraw the virtual text.
-  Vault:  yes, and it is the best case for it. `labels` already has
   `heading_ord`, so inheritance is a recursive lookup of the parent chain in
   SQL, and the picker for `set_tags` lists every label used in the vault with
   counts. Filetags are the labels with a NULL `heading_ord`.
-  After:  II.A.

Done (2026-10-06):

-  Chose:  the existing label tags, as proposed, and the distinction between
   the labels of a file and of a heading is stored in the index. The index
   now records WHAT holds each label (schema version 2, the index is rebuilt
   by itself on the first scan):

| container | what holds the label                                              |
+===========+===================================================================+
| document  | a label tag above the first heading                               |
| data      | a labels key of a data tag (for example the table tag) or of data |
| title     | a label tag in the title of a heading                             |
| body      | a label tag in the tag lines under a heading                      |
| text      | a label tag elsewhere in the text of a section                    |

   `labels` has the columns file_id, heading_ord (empty for a label of the
   file), label, container and line. `tags` got a `region` column (title,
   body, text or document) with the same meaning for any tag.
-  Queries:  `file.labels` is now the labels of the file itself, a new
   `file.heading_labels` holds the labels given inside its headings, and
   `file.tags` / `file.etags` (the Dataview names) and the hash source
   (`FROM #label`) keep counting every label of the file. `heading.labels`
   of a section is unchanged. `file.heading_labels` is a column of database
   views too.
-  API:  `FeyApiFile.labels` (the file itself), `heading_labels`, and
   `label_rows` (every label with `heading_ord`, `container` and `line`).
   `FeyApiVault:labels({ level = 'file' | 'heading', container = ... })` lists
   the labels of one level or one container, with their file counts. SQL
   works too, for example the labels held by headings of the vault:

    ###  src sql
    SELECT label, container, COUNT(*) FROM labels
    WHERE heading_ord IS NOT NULL GROUP BY label, container;
    ###

-  Heading API:  `get_own_tags` (the values of the label tags in the
   metadata region, title or tag lines), `get_tags` (own, with
   `fey_use_tag_inheritance` also those of the headings above and of the
   file), `set_tags` (a list, or a string separated by blanks, `,`, `;` or `:`;
   rewrites the first label tag where it is, removes further ones, adds a tag
   at the end of the title when there is none, removes the tag for an empty
   list), `add_tag`, `remove_tag`, `toggle_tag`, `has_tag`, `is_archived`.
   The labels of the file are `FeyFile:get_filetags`, taken from the same
   extraction as the index.
-  Display:  instead of the virtual text of the proposal, the labels tag is
   concealed to its values like the todo tag (II.E), `work, urgent` at the end
   of the title. `align_tags` is an empty function now, the event listener
   still calls it.
-  Triggers:  `fey_set_tags_command` is enabled as `<prefix>sl`. Not
   `<prefix>t`: that is the table prefix, and a mapping on the prefix would
   wait for the timeout on every table key.
-  Tests:  `tests/labels.lua` (22 checks), `tests/heading_task.lua`.

  II.H. Properties, effort, category, id

-  Now:  `Heading:get_own_properties`, `get_properties`, `set_property`,
   `get_property`, `FeyFile:get_properties`, `set_property`, `get_category`,
   `id_get_or_create`, `Heading:id_get_or_create` and the effort mappings read
   the org `property_drawer`. In the vault the document data holds file level
   properties, but a heading that has subsections cannot hold section data
   (the extractor only records the data of leaf sections).
-  Reuse:  the property inheritance code (`fey_use_property_inheritance`),
   `fey/id.lua` (uuid, timestamp, prefix methods), the `Duration` object.
-  Fey:  tag `prop` (config `fey_property_tag_name`), scope format, key-value
   pairs only, in the heading metadata region. Several tags merge in order.
   File level properties stay as the document data (the table data tag), so
   `FeyFile:get_properties` reads `file.data`. `effort` and `category` are
   conventions on top, not grammar.
-  Triggers:  keybind `fey_set_effort` `<prefix>xe`. `fey_id_*` options stay.
-  Vault:  yes. New column `headings.props` (JSON), filled by the extractor
   from `prop` tags, so "headings where effort is under two hours" works and
   the existing `properties` table keeps the file level keys. The query
   language gets `prop.effort` style fields on tasks.
-  After:  II.A.

Done (2026-10-06):

-  Chose:  the tag `prop` (`fey_property_tag_name`), scope format, key-value
   pairs only, in the metadata region of the heading. Several tags merge, the
   last one wins. Property names are tag keys: lower case, letters, digits and
   `_`, anything else becomes `_` (`header-args` is read and written as
   `header_args`). Values are escaped on write and read back as written.
-  Heading API:  `get_own_properties`, `get_properties` (with
   `fey_use_property_inheritance`), `get_property(name, search_parents)` and
   `set_property(name, value)`. A new key goes into the tag that holds the key,
   else into the first `prop` tag, else into a new tag line directly under the
   heading. A nil or empty value removes the key, and an empty tag with it.
   `get_category` inherits from the headings above, then the file.
-  File:  the properties of a file are the plain values of the document data.
   `FeyFile:get_properties`, `get_property` and `set_property` read and write
   the first `table` tag above the first heading, which is created when there is
   none. This is also where the directives of the file went (III.D, in part):
   `title`, `category`, `todo` (keywords) and `archive` are read from the
   data, `FeyFile:get_data` returns it, and `id_get_or_create` writes `id`.
-  Not done:  the `headings.props` column of the vault (with II.I), the effort
   mapping `fey_set_effort` (it needs the `Duration` code ported with the
   clock, III.A), the header arguments of source blocks (III.I).
-  Tests:  `tests/heading_task.lua`.

Example:

    ###  src fey
      I.A. {# todo, TODO #} Write the report
    {# prop; effort: 2h; id: 6f1c-aa20; category: work #}
    ###


  II.I. Vault task and date index

-  Now:  the vault knows tags, links and labels but not dates or tasks. The
   query engine raises `TASK queries are not supported` and `pages.lua` returns
   an empty `tasks` list.
-  Reuse:  `extract.lua` (tag parse, heading ord assignment), the scan and
   `index_path` pipeline, the schema versioning in `vault.lua`.
-  Fey:  Lua and SQL. Bump `SCHEMA_VERSION` to 2 and add
   Rows with `kind = item` come from list item checkboxes (III.B). Add the
   config lists `vault.date_tags`, `vault.task_tags` next to `label_tags`.
   Add `Vault:index_text(path, lines)` that indexes a buffer without
   saving it. `index_path` reads the disk, so today the index is stale for
   edited buffers. Wire `index_text` to the existing reindex events.
   Extend the query engine: support TASK queries, `tasks` on pages, and date
   fields.
-  Triggers:  autocmd `BufWritePost` already calls `index_path`. New autocmd
   on `TextChanged` and `InsertLeave` with a debounce for `index_text`
   (only for buffers inside an attached vault).
-  Vault:  this is the vault task.
-  After:  II.B, II.C, II.E, II.F, II.H.

Done in part (2026-10-06): the schema is at version 2 with `tags.region` and
`labels.container` / `labels.line` (see II.G). The `dates` and `tasks` tables,
`headings.props` and `Vault:index_text` are still to do.

Done (2026-10-06):

-  Chose:  as proposed, with the names the tags ended up with. Schema version
   3 (an old index is rebuilt by itself). The tables `dates` (file_id,
   heading_ord, line, col, kind, active, start_ts, start_time, end_ts,
   end_time, repeater, warn) and `tasks` (file_id, heading_ord, line, kind,
   state, done, priority, title), and the column `headings.props` (the keys of
   the `prop` tags of a heading, JSON). Dates are epoch seconds in local time;
   `start_time` and `end_time` say whether there is a time of day.
-  Extraction:  `fey.vault.extract` reads every date tag and planning tag with
   `FeyDate.from_parts` (new, needs no buffer), so a range, a time range, a
   repeater (`+1w`, `.+1d`, `++2m`) and a warning (`-3d`, or the `warn` key)
   are all there. A task is a heading whose title starts with a status tag; its
   `state` is the first value, `done` says whether the keyword is a done
   keyword (the keywords of the file win: its `todo` key, else the config), and
   `priority` is the second value or the key. The vault gives the extractor the
   tag names and the keyword lookup, so the extractor itself stays free of config.
-  Vault API:  `Vault:dates(opts)` (`from` and `to` in epoch seconds, `kinds`,
   `active`, `open_only` to leave out the dates of done tasks, `path`; a
   repeating date counts when it started before `to`) with the heading and
   task of each row, and `Vault:tasks(opts)` (`state`, `done`, `priority`,
   `label`, `path`, labels of the heading included). Also on `FeyApiVault`.
-  Unsaved text:  `Vault:index_text(path, lines)` indexes a buffer that was not
   saved. The file keeps the modification time and size of the disk, so a scan
   does not undo it, and saving (`index_path`) or discarding the buffer
   replaces it with the disk. Buffers are indexed after a 300 ms pause on
   `TextChanged` and `InsertLeave` (option `vault.live_index`, default true), and
   `FeyVaultFileIndexed` carries `live = true` for these.
-  Query language:  `page.tasks`; and TASK queries, which were refused before.
   A task has `text`, `state`, `completed`, `priority`, `line`, `file`,
   `signature`, `link`, `labels`, and the dates `scheduled`, `deadline`,
   `closed` and `date`. WHERE, SORT, GROUP BY, LIMIT and FROM (also by label)
   work as for pages. A TASK result is written as a list:
   `-  TODO {link to the heading} (A)`.
-  Not done:  checkbox tasks of list items (they come with III.B, `kind = item`),
   clock dates (III.A), the mappings for effort.
-  Tests:  `tests/index.lua` (40 checks).

Example:

    ###  src sql
    CREATE TABLE dates (
      file_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES files(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
      heading_ord INTEGER, line INTEGER, col INTEGER,
      kind TEXT NOT NULL,        -- date|scheduled|deadline|closed|clock
      active INTEGER NOT NULL,
      start_ts INTEGER, start_time INTEGER,
      end_ts INTEGER, end_time INTEGER,
      repeater TEXT, warn TEXT
    );
    CREATE TABLE tasks (
      file_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES files(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
      heading_ord INTEGER, line INTEGER,
      kind TEXT NOT NULL,        -- heading|item
      state TEXT, done INTEGER, priority TEXT, text TEXT
    );
    ###


  II.I.i. The master vault

This came out of II.I: the data of many vaults has to be one view, for the
agenda most of all. It is still being thought through, so what exists is a
base, and the open questions are at the end.

-  Chose:  the config option is the Fey files directory, `fey_files_dir`
   (default `~/feyfiles`), and it is the master vault. It is created when
   it is missing, with this layout:

    ###  src
    ~/feyfiles/
      agenda/            the agenda files (option master.agenda_dirname)
      .fey/
        vault.db         the index of the master vault, a vault like any other
        dbs/             databases of the master vault (cross vault ones later)
        vaults/          one link per registered vault
    ###

   The option `master` has `enabled`, `agenda_dirname`, `refresh_on_start`,
   `jump_cwd` and `jump_tab`. `fey_agenda_files` is deprecated: it is no
   longer what the agenda reads.
-  Registry:  when a vault is attached (a directory with a `.fey` folder) it
   registers itself with a symbolic link in `.fey/vaults/`, named after its
   directory (`alpha`, then `alpha-2` for a second vault with that name).
   The link points at the vault root and not at the database file: the index
   is `<link>/.fey/vault.db` all the same, the master vault can be browsed
   into other vaults like any folder, and a vault is one thing to move or
   delete. Registering twice does nothing. Without symbolic links (Windows) the
   registration is a `<name>.link` file with the path. A link to a vault that
   is gone stays in the list marked as not available, until `:FeyVaultsPrune`.
   The master vault does not scan the links (hidden directories are skipped).
-  Merged view:  `fey.vault.master` (and `require('fey.api').master()`):
   `vaults()`, `dates(opts)`, `tasks(opts)`, `labels(opts)`, `query(sql)`,
   `abs(vault, path)`, `revision()`, `refresh()`. The rows of every vault come
   back together, each with `vault` (the name; the master vault is `master`)
   and `abs` (the file to write to); dates are ordered by start. Nothing is
   copied and nothing is attached: every vault keeps its own index and the
   master opens them. SQLite attaches 10 databases at most by default, ids
   collide between vaults, and a copy would duplicate the data and need a
   sync, so the merge is done per vault and put together in Lua.
-  Keeping them current:  the master index and, with `refresh_on_start`, the
   index of every registered vault are updated in the background at startup,
   one after the other. A file saved in a vault this session did not open is
   found through the registry and indexed. Edited buffers are indexed live
   (II.I), in whichever vault they belong to.
-  Jumping:  `:FeyVaults [tab] [cwd|nocwd]` picks a vault and opens it; the keys
   `<prefix>vj` and `<prefix>vJ` (in a new tab). Jumping sets the working
   directory (`tcd` in a new tab, `cd` otherwise, so the vault attaches), unless
   `master.jump_cwd` is off, and opens the vault directory.
-  Editing:  rows of the merged view say where their file is, so a write goes
   to the right vault: the code that writes a cell, opens a note or reads a
   file date asks the vault for the path (`vault:abs(path)`) instead of joining
   the root itself.
-  Tests:  `tests/master.lua` (55 checks).
-  Open questions:
   1. The query language and the database views still see one vault. For the
      merged view the page needs a vault: `FROM` could take a prefix
      (`FROM "alpha:notes"`), or a database could have a scope (this vault, all
      vaults, a list). Which?
   2. How are links between vaults written, `{@ link, alpha:notes/a.fey @}`?
   3. The agenda reads the master view. Should every registered vault show up,
      or can a vault opt out (a flag in its own `.fey`), and may a database or
      agenda be limited to a list of vaults?
   4. Editing a cell of a merged database works through the path of the row
      (the code is ready for it). Should an unsaved buffer of that file be
      written to instead of the file, as it is for one vault now?
   5. Is `~/feyfiles` the right default, and is `master` the right name for the
      merged view in the code and the docs?

Decided and built (2026-10-06): the answers to the questions above, with what
they turned into. The text above stays for the history; where it disagrees,
this is what is true.

-  Vaults nest:  a vault is any directory with a `.fey` folder, also one inside
   another vault. The files of a vault are the Fey files below its root up to
   the first directory that has a `.fey` of its own: scanning stops there, and
   the parent neither indexes nor reacts to the files of the vault inside it.
   Module `fey.vault.tree`.
-  Registration:  a vault registers with its NEAREST ancestor vault, the one it
   lives in, found by looking up the file system tree to the root of the drive.
   Only a vault with no vault above it registers with the master vault. Every
   vault has its own registry, `<vault>/.fey/vaults/`, with one link per vault
   directly below it. Attaching a vault also registers the vaults above it, so
   the chain from the master vault is whole. A vault created above existing
   ones takes them over: their old registration in the master vault is
   marked misplaced and left out, they re-register with the new vault the next
   time they attach, and `:FeyVaultsPrune` (now for every registry in the tree)
   removes the stale links.
-  Names and ids:  a name is unique in its registry. `:FeyVaultInit [name]`
   asks for it (the directory name is the default, a taken name is refused and
   asked again), and keeps it in the vault's own `.fey/vault.fey`, a Fey data
   file with one table tag, so the id does not change when the vault is
   registered again. The id of a vault is the chain of names from the master
   vault: `master`, `master:notes`, `master:notes:history`.
-  Settings:  `.fey/vault.fey` has `name` and `merge`. `merge: false` is the
   opt-out: the vault, and everything below it, stays out of the merged views
   of the vaults above it (the `tree` and `master` scopes, so the master
   agenda). It is called merge and not agenda because it governs databases,
   queries and labels too. The vault still sees itself and its own tree. A
   list scope that names the vault includes it. `:FeyVaultMerge [on|off]`
   flips it for the vault of the working directory.
-  References:  a keyword, the names down to the vault, and the file after the
   first `/`: `master:notes:history/a.fey`, `current:sub/b.fey` (`current` is the
   vault the reference is written in), `master/agenda/inbox.fey` for a file of
   the master vault, `master:notes` for the vault alone. A text that does not
   start with `master` or `current` is a plain path, so a folder that is really
   named master is written `./master/x.fey`.
-  Links between vaults:  `{@ link, master:notes:history/a.fey @}`, also in a
   `section` tag and with the `section` key. The index keeps the vault part of
   the link in `links.target_ref` (schema version 4) next to the path, and
   resolves it when it is used, so a link follows a vault that moved. Opening
   such a link opens the file; a link to a vault alone jumps to the vault.
   Backlinks work over a scope: `vault:foreign_backlinks`, and
   `scope.backlinks(spec, current, target_root, path)` which also finds the
   links that name the vault. Not built: a rename of a vault does not rewrite
   the references to its old id.
-  Scopes:  `current` (default), `tree` (this vault and every vault below it),
   `master` (all of them), or a list of references, which can end in `:*` for a
   vault and what is below it (`master:notes:*`). Module `fey.vault.scope`:
   `resolve`, `dates`, `tasks`, `labels`, `query`, `backlinks`, `revision`. The
   agenda will take the same scope: its own vault, the master agenda, or the
   vault and the vaults below it.
-  Queries and databases over a scope:  the query language reads a merged
   store of the stores of those vaults, so pages are the pages of their own
   vault. A `query` tag (and the API) takes a scope: `{# query, TABLE rating;
   scope: master #}`, with a list written separated by blanks, `scope: master:a
   master:b:*`. A database has a `scope` key in its own file, and the view has
   an action for it (`gs`, or the palette) and shows it in the status line.
   New field `file.vault` (the id), a column of database views too. A link
   object carries the vault of its file, and is written with it when it points
   into another vault than the file the result is written in
   (`master:beta/b.fey`); links compare equal when the path matches and the
   vaults do not disagree, so `contains(file.outlinks, this.file.link)` still
   works. `FROM` is worked out inside each vault: a label or a folder matches
   in every vault, and `incoming` / `outgoing` follow links inside a vault only.
-  Editing:  a cell of a row of another vault is written to the file of that
   vault, and to the unsaved buffer when the file is open in one: the buffer
   is changed and stays unsaved, the file on the disk is not touched. The user
   is told about unsaved changes by Neovim when quitting.
-  Names:  `feyfiles` and `master` stay. If a name from the world of the fey is
   wanted, `court` (the fey court that holds the vaults) or `hollow` fit; the
   keyword is one word in `fey.vault.tree` and the docs, so changing it later is
   cheap.
-  Tests:  `tests/tree.lua` (98 checks), `tests/scope_query.lua` (45),
   `tests/master.lua` (55).
-  Open:  1. The agenda option for its default scope (planned `fey_agenda_scope`,
   default master) comes with II.J. 2. A command to rename a vault and rewrite
   the references to it. 3. `FROM "master:notes"` for a vault inside a query
   (the scope covers the need today). 4. The jump picker lists every vault; it
   could group them as a tree.

Renamed, and the navigator as the jump picker (2026-10-06): the words changed,
and the picker is now the navigator. The text above uses the old words, read
`master vault` as court, and `vault` where it means a directory as hollow.

-  Words:  a hollow is a directory with a `.fey` folder (hollows nest), its
   vault is the database in that folder, and the court is the master hollow,
   the top of the tree. So: `fey_files_dir` is `fey_court_dir` (default
   `~/feyhollow`) and the option table `master` is `court`; the keyword of a
   reference is `court` (`court:notes:history/a.fey`, `court/agenda/inbox.fey`,
   scope `court`); the registry of a hollow is `.fey/hollows/` and its settings
   file `.fey/hollow.fey`; modules `fey.hollow.tree`, `fey.hollow.scope`,
   `fey.hollow.court` and `require('fey.api').court()`; commands
   `:FeyHollowInit`, `:FeyHollows`, `:FeyHollowsPrune`, `:FeyHollowMerge`
   (`:FeyVaultReindex` stays, it reindexes a vault); mappings
   `fey_hollow_init`, `fey_hollow_jump`, `fey_hollow_jump_tab`. A row, a page
   or a link says which hollow it is in: `file.hollow`, `link.hollow`,
   `row.hollow`, `hollows` in the label lists; `vault` is only ever the
   database, as in `vault_of(row)` and `vault:query`.
-  Navigator:  the jump picker is the navigator view, extended in three ways.
   1. A thin pane on the left lists the level above, with the place we came
      from marked (options `show_parent` and `parent_width`).
   2. `h` at the top of a document leaves it for its directory. From there `h`
      climbs the directories to the root of the hollow, goes on through the
      directories of the hollow it lives in when it is a subhollow, and from a
      hollow with no hollow above it up into the list of hollows of the court
      (the cursor on it), then to the court itself. `l` goes back down; on a
      file it opens the document, with the place in it kept. A file that was
      only loaded to look at it is unloaded again when the navigator closes.
   3. `H` switches to a level that lists only hollows, next to the hollow of
      what is shown; there `l` goes to the hollows below one, `h` to the
      hollows next to its parent, up to the court.
   `<CR>` jumps: to an object, to a file, or, on a hollow, to the hollow
   (`court.jump_cwd`, `court.jump_tab`), and `<C-t>` jumps in a new tab. The
   preview shows a file, the directory listing, or what a hollow is (id, path,
   its vault, the hollows below it, whether it is in merged views).
-  Opening:  `<prefix>;` opens it on the current file (and, as before, `<prefix>:`
   where it was left; both now follow `mappings.prefix`). `<prefix>vj` and
   `<prefix>vJ` (new tab), `:FeyHollows [tab] [cwd|nocwd]` and
   `:FeyNavigate hollows` open it straight on the hollows. In a buffer that is
   not a Fey file it opens on the directory of the file.
-  Tests:  `tests/navigator.lua` (75 checks), and the other suites with the new
   names.

  II.J. Agenda core

-  Now:  `agenda/init.lua`, `agenda/types/*`, `agenda_item.lua`, `filter.lua`,
   `sorting_strategy.lua` and `view/*` build items from `FeyHeading` objects of
   `FeyFiles` (every file listed in `fey_agenda_files`, loaded with tree-sitter
   at startup). Every `FeyHeading` method it needs is part of II.C to II.H.
-  Reuse:  the view layer (`line.lua`, `token.lua`, highlighting), the sorting
   strategy, the filter language, the day grouping, the warning days,
   time grid, repeater expansion in `types/agenda.lua`. These work on
   `FeyDate` values and item fields, not on nodes.
-  Fey:  Lua only. `FeyAgendaItem` is built from vault rows: file, line,
   heading title, state, priority, labels, category, effort, and one date.
   `FeyAgenda:new` takes the vault instead of `FeyFiles`; the agenda scope is
   the vault (optionally narrowed by a label or a path glob in the custom
   command). `fey_agenda_files` is deprecated and only kept as a path filter.
   Repeating dates (`dates.repeater` not NULL) are loaded in full and expanded
   with `FeyDate:repeats_on`; everything else is selected by `start_ts` range.
-  Triggers:  keybind `fey_agenda` `<prefix>a` (global), the user command
   `:Fey agenda` (already generated in `global.lua`). Autocmd on
   `User FeyVaultFileIndexed` and `FeyVaultIndexed` redraws an open agenda, like
   `db/view.lua` already does.
-  Vault:  yes, it is the data source. Faster startup (no tree-sitter parse of
   every agenda file in `Fey:init`), agenda across the whole vault, and the
   query tag `TASK` can render the same lists inside a note.
-  After:  II.I.

Changed (2026-10-06): the agenda reads the master view (II.I.i), not one vault
and not `fey_agenda_files`. It takes a scope: the vault itself, `tree`, or
`master`, which is the default, and a vault that set `merge: false` stays out
of the master agenda. A project keeps its agenda in its own vault and it
shows in the global agenda; the agenda of the vault of the working directory
is the same query limited to that vault. Items carry the vault name and the
path of their file, so the actions of II.L write to the right vault. The data
comes from `master.dates` and `master.tasks`, repeating dates are expanded in
Lua from the rows that have a repeater.

Done (2026-10-06): the agenda reads the vaults.

-  Chose:  an agenda entry (`agenda/entry.lua`) is a heading read from a vault
   row. It answers what the views ask of a heading (`get_todo`, `is_done`,
   `get_priority`, `get_priority_sort_value`, `get_title`, `get_category`,
   `get_tags`, `has_tag`, `tags_to_string`, `matches_category`) and knows where
   it lives (`hollow`, `path`, `abs`, `line`, `ord`, `signature`), so the
   sorting strategy, the filters and the line builder did not change. The
   category is the `category` prop of the heading, else the `category` of
   the document, else the file name. Labels are the tags of the filters.
-  Source:  `agenda/source.lua` builds the entries and their `FeyDate` values
   from `scope.dates`. It reads open planning dates up to the end of the
   range (an overdue scheduled item stays on the agenda, a deadline shows
   ahead of time), plain dates and ranges that overlap the range, and
   everything that repeats. A date range becomes the start and the end,
   linked, as before. The scope is `fey_agenda_scope`, default `court`, and
   falls back to `current` when the court is switched off. A source also takes
   `scope`, `root` (the hollow `current` and `tree` start from, default the
   hollow of the working directory) and `paths`.
-  Vault:  `FeyVault:dates` rows now carry `heading_line`, `props`,
   `category` (of the document) and `labels` (of the heading).
-  Changed:  `Agenda:new` takes a `source` and passes it to the views. A view
   reads its days from `source:dates`, so there is no file loading left in the
   agenda view: `redo` has nothing to reload. A custom command with
   `fey_agenda_files` limits the source to those files and directories
   (globs and `~` work), and so does the global `fey_agenda_files`, which is
   deprecated for that use only. Titles of items are drawn without the
   markup highlights, since there is no tree to take them from.
-  Tests:  `tests/agenda.lua` (54 checks): entries, days with deadlines,
   scheduled, repeaters, ranges and times, scopes (current, tree, list,
   court, opt-out), path filters, label and category filters, priority
   sorting, rendering into a buffer.
-  Not yet:  the other views (`todo`, `tags`, `tags_todo`, `search`) still
   read `FeyFiles`, they follow with II.K. The actions on a line
   (II.L: goto, todo, priority, dates, refile) and the clock still need a
   heading object that can write, so they keep the old code until then.
   `fey_agenda_scope` as a prompt key is not built.
-  Mappings outside of documents:  the mappings that make sense anywhere moved
   from the `fey` group (documents only) to the `global` group: `fey_agenda`
   (`<prefix>a`, the agenda prompt), `fey_hollow_init` (`<prefix>vi`),
   `fey_vault_reindex` (`<prefix>vr`), `fey_hollow_jump` and `fey_hollow_jump_tab`
   (`<prefix>vj`, `<prefix>vJ`) and the database ones (`<prefix>bn`, `bs`, `bv`,
   `bt`, `bl`). The query and tag mappings stay with documents. A user mapping
   for one of them goes under `mappings.global` now. `fey_capture` stays off
   until II.M.
-  Details on their own lines:  the title and the item line stay clean. A line
   under the title says where the agenda looks (`Scope: court (4 hollows)`,
   `fey_agenda_show_scope`), and under each item a line says its hollow and file
   (`court:alpha:child · c.fey`, `fey_agenda_show_hollow`: `auto` is when the
   agenda reads more than one hollow, or `always`, `never`). The line has the
   same heading as the item, so the actions work on either. Both use the
   highlight `@fey.agenda.hollow`, linked to Comment. The court counts as a
   hollow in the count.

  II.K. Agenda views and the match language

-  Now:  `types/agenda.lua`, `todo.lua`, `tags.lua`, `tags_todo.lua`,
   `search.lua` and `files/elements/search.lua` (the `+work-home/TODO` match
   syntax, the property comparison operators, the regex terms).
-  Reuse:  the match syntax parser, which is plain text and independent of
   the tree; only the evaluator that reads heading fields changes.
-  Fey:  Lua. Evaluate a match against item rows: a label means a vault label,
   `PROP=value` means `headings.props`, `TODO` means `tasks.state`. Compile the
   match string to a WHERE clause where it can, so the filtering happens
   in SQL. Custom commands (`fey_agenda_custom_commands`) may also hold a query
   language string, which makes a saved agenda view an ordinary `TASK` query.
   Agenda prompt keys `a`, `t`, `m`, `M`, `s` stay.
-  Triggers:  the agenda prompt menu, keybinds `fey_agenda_filter` `/`,
   `fey_agenda_day_view` `vd` and the other span keys.
-  Vault:  yes (above).
-  After:  II.J.

  II.L. Agenda buffer actions

-  Now:  the commented `agenda` mappings in `config/defaults.lua`
   (later/earlier, views, goto, switch to, redo, todo, priority, tags,
   deadline, schedule, refile, archive, clock, effort, note, preview,
   filter, clockreport) call `Heading` methods through an agenda item that
   holds a `FeyHeading`.
-  Reuse:  the whole mapping table and the action wiring.
-  Fey:  every action loads the item's file buffer, finds the heading by line
   (`FeyFile:get_closest_heading` or by `ord`), performs the change with the
   Heading API of II.C to II.H, then re-indexes with `index_text` and redraws.
   Nothing in the agenda edits text itself.
-  Triggers:  uncomment the `agenda` mapping group one action at a time, in the
   order: goto, switch_to, redo, todo, priority, deadline, schedule, set_tags,
   refile, archive, clock, effort, note, preview.
-  Vault:  the redraw after an edit is the cheap incremental reindex of one
   file, not a reload of all files.
-  After:  II.J, II.K.

  II.M. Public agenda API and positions

-  Now:  `api/agenda.lua` calls `fey.agenda:agenda/todos/tags/tags_todo` with
   options that still reference org config names, and its
   `get_heading_at_cursor` uses `require('fey.api').load` and
   `file:get_heading_on_line`, neither of which exists in the new API.
   `api/position.lua` (`FeyPosition`) has no user left.
-  Reuse:  option table shape, `FeyApiAgendaOptions` types, the date argument
   handling.
-  Fey:  Lua. Rebuild on the vault API: `get_heading_at_cursor` returns a
   `FeyApiHeading` by `file` plus `line`, add `FeyApiFile:heading_at(line)`
   (the data is already in `file.headings`). Replace `FeyPosition` with the
   `line`/`end_line` pair that `FeyApiHeading` already has, or delete
   `position.lua` once the agenda items carry lines. Add `FeyApiVault:tasks(opts)`
   and `FeyApiVault:dates(opts)` returning the rows of II.I.
-  Triggers:  none. Update the generated docs (`doc/fey_api.txt`).
-  Vault:  yes (see above).
-  After:  II.I, II.J.

  II.N. Refile

-  Now:  `capture/init.lua` refile functions (`refile`, `_refile_from_fey_file`,
   `_refile_from_capture_buffer`, `get_destination`, `autocomplete_refile`,
   `_adapt_heading_level`) and the mappings `fey_refile` `<prefix>r`,
   `fey_capture_refile`, `fey_agenda_refile` (commented out). The destination
   prompt lists headings from the loaded `FeyFiles`.
-  Reuse:  the level adaptation (`_adapt_heading_level`), the empty line
   trimming around ranges, the cross-file `update` plumbing, `ui/input`,
   `ui/fuzzy`. Heading moves are already native (`move_subtree_*` and
   `HeadingMoved`).
-  Fey:  Lua. Source: the heading at the cursor, or the list item subtree
   (`ts_utils.closest_listitem_node`). Destination: pick from the vault, not
   from loaded files. The picker is `ui/fuzzy` over a query on
   `headings` (path, signature, title), grouped by file. The moved text
   goes through the existing reindexing (`reindex_headings`) so signatures
   renumber in the destination and the source. Refiling a heading does
   not leave a link behind unless asked: option `fey_refile_leave_link` writes a
   `section` tag (`FeyApi.insert_section_link`) at the old place. The org
   `fey_refile_targets` notion becomes a vault query string, default all
   files.
-  Triggers:  keybind `fey_refile` `<prefix>r` in fey buffers,
   `fey_agenda_refile` in the agenda, `fey_capture_refile` in capture.
   Autocmd: none. Dispatch `HeadingMoved` after.
-  Vault:  yes. Destination candidates and a "recent destinations" list come
   from the index. Inbound `link` and `section` tags to the moved heading are
   found through `Vault:backlinks` and offered for rewriting. This is a
   real improvement over org, which left them dangling. The file move
   updates `links.target_file` when the heading changes file.
-  After:  II.A.

  II.O. Archive

-  Now:  `Capture:refile_file_heading_to_archive`, `FeyMappings:archive`,
   `toggle_archive_tag`, `Heading:is_archived`, `FeyFile:get_archive_file_location`,
   the default `fey_archive_location = '%s_archive::'`, and the `.fey_archive`
   file type.
-  Reuse:  the refile code (II.N), the archive file naming helper.
-  Fey:  the label `archive` is the archive marker (a `labels` tag, II.G).
   The location option drops the org `::heading` suffix: it is a path
   template, `%s_archive.fey_archive`. Archiving writes a `prop` tag
   `archived_from; file: ..; path: ..; at: ..` on the moved heading, so it can
   be restored.
-  Triggers:  keybinds `fey_archive_subtree` `<prefix>$`, `fey_toggle_archive_tag`
   `<prefix>A`, `fey_agenda_archive`.
-  Vault:  yes. `.fey_archive` files are already indexed, and the archived
   flag becomes a normal label filter in queries and agendas
   (`fey_agenda_skip_archived`).
-  After:  II.G, II.H, II.N.

  II.P. Capture

-  Now:  `capture/init.lua`, `capture/template/*`, `capture/templates.lua`,
   `datetree.lua`. Templates are org text: the default is `'* TODO %?\n  %u'`
   and the destination is a headline path or a datetree. The mapping
   `fey_capture` is commented out.
-  Reuse:  the capture window, template expansion (`%?`, `%u`, `%t`, `%^{}`
   prompts), the datetree code (it only needs fey signatures), the menu.
-  Fey:  Lua and templates. Rewrite the default template as Fey text with the
   new tags, with new expansion keys for them.
   A new target type `file` plus `heading signature or title`, and `query` (a
   query string selecting the target heading). The datetree builds Fey
   headings by numbering `I.`, `I.A.`, `I.A.i.` with the configured segment
   order instead of stars.
-  Triggers:  keybind `fey_capture` `<prefix>c`, capture window mappings
   `fey_capture_finalize` `<C-c>`, `fey_capture_kill` `<prefix>k`.
   Autocmd: `BufWritePost` of the capture buffer.
-  Vault:  yes. Offer targets from the vault, and optionally add a template
   key `unique` that refuses a title already present in the index.
-  After:  II.E, II.N.

Example:

    ###  src lua
    fey_capture_templates = {
      t = {
        description = 'Task',
        template = '  I. {# todo, TODO #} %?\n{# scheduled, %t #}',
      },
    }
    ###


  II.Q. Notifications

-  Now:  `notifications/init.lua` gets tasks from `FeyFiles`, uses `Date`,
   and runs on a one minute timer. `Fey.cron` runs it headless.
-  Reuse:  the timer, popup, system notification, the reminder time config.
-  Fey:  Lua. `get_tasks(time)` becomes a SQL query on `dates` (active, not
   done, start within the reminder window), including expanded repeaters.
-  Triggers:  autocmd: start the timer from `Fey.setup` when
   `notifications.enabled`. The headless cron entry point stays.
-  Vault:  yes. Because it needs no buffer, it runs fine in the cron mode
   where the vault is just the database file.
-  After:  II.I.


  II.R. Phase 1 documentation checkpoint

-  Now:  the docs describe the org plugin: `doc/fey.txt` and `docs/*.org` know
   nothing of headings with tags, dates, planning tags, status, labels,
   properties, the agenda over a vault, refile or capture. `README.fey` has a
   feature section only for what was ported first. The tutorial of the test
   vault documents `file.labels` as "labels as written" and lists the tag names
   of the index.
-  Reuse:  the structure of `README.fey` (section II, the feature set), the
   generators `scripts/gendoc.lua` and `scripts/build_docs.sh`.
-  Fey:  no syntax. One pass, after II.Q, over everything phase 1 changed:
   `README.fey` gets a section for each of the date tags, the planning tags,
   status (keyword and priority), labels and their containers, properties,
   the agenda, refile, archive, capture and notifications, each with its keys
   and an example. `doc/fey_api.txt` is regenerated (the API changed: labels,
   heading labels, the agenda API). The vault tutorial gets the new fields
   (`file.heading_labels`, the `container` of a label, the new tables).
   The list of mappings in the README is made from `config/defaults.lua`, not
   written by hand.
-  Triggers:  none. Run `scripts/build_docs.sh`.
-  Vault:  yes, the tutorial vault is both the documentation and the fixture of
   the index tests, so it is checked by running its live tags.
-  After:  II.Q.


  III. Phase 2: the rest of the conversion

  III.A. Clock and logbook

-  Now:  `clock/init.lua`, `clock/report.lua`, `files/elements/logbook.lua`,
   the events `ClockedIn`/`ClockedOut` and the `ClockOut` listener. The
   logbook is an org drawer of `CLOCK: [ts]--[ts] => h:mm` lines.
-  Reuse:  `Logbook` arithmetic (totals, active clock), `Duration`, the
   report formatting, the statusline (`fey.statusline()`).
-  Fey:  tag `clock` (config `fey_clock_tag_name`) inside a pair tag
   `logbook` in the heading body. The `clock` tag has two `date` values
   and a computed duration key-value written on clock out.
   The open clock has no `end`. The reader is `Logbook.from_tags`. The
   `fey_log_into_drawer` option becomes `fey_log_into_logbook` (a boolean).
-  Triggers:  keybinds `fey_clock_in` `<prefix>xi`, `fey_clock_out` `<prefix>xo`,
   `fey_clock_cancel` `<prefix>xq`, `fey_clock_goto` `<prefix>xj`. Autocmd:
   clock out when the todo state changes to a done state (existing listener),
   and `VimLeavePre` to warn about an open clock.
-  Vault:  yes. Index `clock` tags as `dates` with `kind = clock`, so total
   time per heading, per label and per day is an aggregate query, and the
   clock report in the agenda no longer needs to parse logbooks.
-  After:  II.E, II.I.

Example:

    ###  src fey
    [ logbook #]
    {# clock, 2026-10-06 Tue 10:00; end: 2026-10-06 Tue 11:30; dur: 1:30 #}
    [# logbook ]
    ###


  III.B. Checkboxes and progress cookies

-  Now:  `files/elements/listitem.lua` (`checkbox`, `update_checkbox`,
   `child_checkboxes`, cookie updating), `Heading:update_cookie`,
   `FeyMappings:toggle_checkbox`.
-  Reuse:  state computation (on/off/children), the cookie arithmetic, the
   whole checkbox propagation to parent items and headings.
-  Fey:  grammar rule. In `listitem` add `optional(field('checkbox',
   $.checkbox))` after the bullet separator.
   No scanner change is expected: the pair tag opener is decided by scanner
   lookahead (open bracket, blank, name, blank, closing token and bracket), and
   the checkbox shape fails that check, but the longest match between the
   checkbox token and the pair tag start token must be verified, and a corpus test added. Cookies (one over three, or a
   percentage) stay plain text matched by a Lua pattern, no grammar.
-  Triggers:  keybind `fey_toggle_checkbox` `<C-Space>`. Autocmd: none; the
   cookie update runs from the mapping.
-  Vault:  yes. Checked and unchecked items are `tasks` rows with
   `kind = item` (II.I), which unlocks TASK queries over checklists, and
   the percent done of a heading as a computed field.
-  After:  II.I.

Example:

    ###  src js
    checkbox: $ => choice(
      token(prec(2, '[ ]')),
      seq(
        token(prec(2, '[')),
        field('status', alias(token.immediate(/[xX-]/), $.status)),
        token.immediate(']'),
      ),
    ),
    ###


  III.C. Footnotes

-  Now:  `objects/footnote.lua`, `FeyFile:find_footnote_definition`,
   `find_footnote_reference`, `FeyMappings:_jump_to_footnote_*`. The
   grammar has no `fndef` or `fnref` (the rule is commented out).
-  Reuse:  the `FeyFootnote` object, the jump and creation logic.
-  Fey:  tags, no grammar. Reference: tag `fn`, scope format, one value, the
   label. Definition: pair tag `fn` whose body is the text. Handler
   `scope_tag`: jump to the definition; `pair_tag`: jump back to the first
   reference.
-  Triggers:  `fey_open_at_point` `<prefix>o` (already generic over tag
   handlers). Insert mapping `<prefix>nf` creates a reference and a definition.
-  Vault:  small. A `footnote` is a tag, so a missing definition is a query
   (`tags` where `name = 'fn'` without a matching pair). Add it to the health
   check.
-  After:  II.A.

Example:

    ###  src fey
    The result is odd {@ fn, 1 @}.

    [ fn, 1 #]
    Measured twice.
    [# fn ]
    ###


  III.D. Document directives become data

-  Now:  `FeyFile:get_filetags`, `get_title`, `get_category`,
   `_get_directive`, `get_directive_properties`, `get_header_args`,
   `get_todo_keywords` (reads `#+todo`), `id_get_or_create`, and the
   completion source `directives.lua`. There are no `#+name:` lines in Fey.
-  Reuse:  the call sites, which only need a different source.
-  Fey:  no new syntax. The directive list becomes the document data
   (the table data tag): `title`, `category`, `todo` (keywords), `header_args`,
   and the `labels` tag for filetags. `FeyFile:get_directive(name)` becomes
   `file.data[name]`, and `get_todo_keywords` reads `data.todo` with the config
   default.
   Table formulas (`#+tblfm`): there was no formula engine in orgmode either.
   If wanted later, a tag `tblfm` directly after a table.
-  Triggers:  none. `FileType` and reindex events already refresh buffers.
-  Vault:  yes, fully: the document data is already in `files.data` and
   `properties`. Delete the buffer-side parsing.
-  After:  nothing.

Done in part (2026-10-06): `FeyFile:_get_directive` reads keys of the document
data now, so `title`, `category`, `todo` and `archive` work (see II.H). The
file labels come from the index extraction. Not done: `filetags` as a data key
(labels do that job), the header arguments (III.I) and the table formulas.

  III.E. Drawers and notes

-  Now:  `Heading:get_drawer`, `get_drawer_append_line`, `add_to_drawer`,
   `add_note`, `FeyMappings:add_note`, `capture:build_note_capture`,
   `ts_utils.is_date_in_drawer`.
-  Reuse:  the note capture window, the note event and its listener.
-  Fey:  generalise to a named pair tag. A drawer is a pair tag whose name is
   the drawer name, a note is a list item with a `date` tag inside the `logbook` pair tag
   (III.A). `get_drawer(name)` finds the pair tag of that name in the heading
   body; fold support for pair tags already exists in `fey/folds.lua`.
-  Triggers:  keybind `fey_add_note` `<prefix>na`, `fey_note_finalize` `<C-c>`,
   `fey_note_kill` `<prefix>k`.
-  Vault:  small. Notes are found by `tags` rows named `logbook`.
-  After:  III.A.

  III.F. Comments

-  Now:  `fey_comment_tag_name = 'comment'` exists in the config but there is
   no handler. `commentstring` is still the org `# %s`.
-  Reuse:  the vim commenting commands, which only need a string.
-  Fey:  tag, no grammar. A comment is a line tag or a block tag named
   `comment`. Set `commentstring` to the line tag form and add the capture
   `@comment` to `highlights.scm` for the `comment` tag name so the
   whole body is dimmed. The extractor skips the body of comment tags when
   it looks for links, labels and tasks.
-  Triggers:  `gc` and friends through `commentstring`.
-  Vault:  yes, minor: do not index tags inside a comment.
-  After:  nothing.

  III.G. Dynamic blocks and result tags

-  Now:  org dynamic blocks (`#+BEGIN: clocktable`) have no equivalent, and
   `capture`, the clock report and `Heading:add_to_drawer` assume them.
-  Reuse:  `query_result` and `feydb_result` writing in `fey.query`, the
   clock report builders in `clock/report.lua`.
-  Fey:  tags, no grammar. The query tag already is the Fey dynamic block:
   a block or pair tag that regenerates a `*_result` pair tag. Add a tag
   `clocktable` with the same lifecycle (head parameters: `span`, `scope`,
   `by`), written into `clocktable_result`. It is a thin wrapper that builds
   a query over `dates` with `kind = clock`.
-  Triggers:  the existing query mappings `<prefix>qq` and `<prefix>qa`, and
   `run_on_load`.
-  Vault:  yes, entirely (it is a query).
-  After:  III.A.

  III.H. LaTeX

-  Now:  `colors/highlighter/markup/latex.lua`, the option
   `fey_highlight_latex_and_related` and the ftplugin `syntax` switch refer to
   `latex_env`, which the fey grammar does not have.
-  Reuse:  the latex injection setup.
-  Fey:  no grammar. A display equation is a fenced block named `latex` (or
   `math`) and is injected with the latex parser through `injections.scm`.
   Inline math is a line tag named `math`, also injected. The `latex` hl
   code only needs the new capture names.
-  Triggers:  none, it is highlighting.
-  Vault:  none.
-  After:  nothing.

  III.I. Source blocks, edit special and babel

-  Now:  edit special (`objects/edit_special`) and `babel/tangle.lua` read
   `block` fields. The fey `block` has `name` and `parameter` fields, so edit
   special mostly works; header arguments (`:tangle`, `:noweb`) are parsed
   from the parameter text and from the removed `#+property` line, and
   the `indentkeys` in the ftplugin still mention `end_src` and
   `end_example`.
-  Reuse:  almost everything. Block parsing (`block.lua`), tangle writing,
   noweb expansion, the temporary edit window.
-  Fey:  Lua. Read header arguments from the block parameters and from the
   document data key `header_args` (III.D). Fix the stale `indentkeys`
   and the `fey_edit_src_*` options. Verify edit special inside list items and
   pair tags (fence indentation rules).
-  Triggers:  keybinds `fey_edit_special` `<prefix>'`, `fey_babel_tangle`
   `<prefix>bt` (conflicts with `fey_db_new_tab`; pick a new default).
-  Vault:  yes. Index blocks (language, `tangle` target, name) in a `blocks`
   table. Then "tangle the whole vault", detection of two blocks that write the
   same file, and a noweb check that every reference resolves.
-  After:  III.D.

  III.J. Links consolidation

-  Now:  two stacks exist side by side. The new one (`fey.links`, the `link`
   and `section` tags, `FeyApi.insert_link`) and the old one
   (`fey/fey/links`, `fey/fey/hyperlinks`, `FeyLinks` with the file, id,
   headline, url, custom_id types, `Fey.links`). The mappings `insert_link`
   `<prefix>li` and `store_link` `<prefix>ls` are commented out.
-  Reuse:  the url type (plain urls in text), custom hyperlink sources
   (`hyperlinks.sources`), `FeyLinkUrl`.
-  Fey:  Lua. Remove the file, id, headline and custom_id types. An id
   link becomes a `link` tag whose target is a `prop` id (II.H) resolved
   through the vault. Keep url handling for bare urls: `open_at_cursor` checks
   the word under the cursor when there is no tag. Retire `Fey.links`.
-  Triggers:  keybinds `fey_insert_link` `<prefix>li`, `fey_store_link`
   `<prefix>ls`, `fey_open_at_point` `<prefix>o`.
-  Vault:  yes. Pickers list files and headings from the index, links are
   validated against `files` and `headings`, and a broken link report is a
   query on `links` where `target_file` is NULL.
-  After:  II.H.

  III.K. Completion

-  Now:  the sources `directives`, `hyperlinks`, `plan`, `properties`, `tags`,
   `todo_keywords` in `fey/autocompletion` are org specific (they match `#+`,
   `:`, `*` contexts). The omnifunc, cmp and blink adapters remain.
-  Reuse:  the adapter layer (omnifunc, cmp, blink) and the fuzzy matching.
-  Fey:  Lua. Replace the sources by tag-aware ones that decide the context
   from the tree: tag name (from the vault `tags` table, with counts), the
   value after `,` (todo keywords for `todo`, labels for `labels`, file paths
   for `link`, signatures for `section`), and the key after `;` (known keys
   per tag name, collected from the index).
-  Triggers:  `omnifunc`, and the cmp or blink trigger characters `;`, `,`
   and the open of a scope tag.
-  Vault:  yes, it is the main data source.
-  After:  II.A, II.G.

  III.L. Highlighting, concealment and folds

-  Now:  `queries/fey/highlights.scm` already colours the tag formats, and
   `colors/highlighter/todos.lua` (keyword faces) and `foldtext.lua` still look
   for org nodes. `highlights.scm.org.bak` is the old query. `fey_ellipsis`,
   `fey_hide_emphasis_markers` and the folds are partly done.
-  Reuse:  face definitions and the foldtext builder.
-  Fey:  queries only. Capture the tag name of `todo`, `priority`, `scheduled`,
   `deadline`, `date` so they take the todo and date faces, and conceal
   their syntax when `fey_conceal_task_tags` is set (II.E). Overdue
   deadlines are highlighted from the index through an extmark pass
   run after reindex. Delete `highlights.scm.org.bak` when done.
-  Triggers:  autocmd `ColorScheme` (existing), reindex events.
-  Vault:  small, for the overdue pass.
-  After:  II.C, II.E.

  III.M. Heading commands audit

-  Now:  `toggle_heading`, `insert_heading_from_plain_line`, `insert_todo_heading*`,
   `cycle` and `global_cycle`, `fey_startup_folded`, `next/previous_visible_heading`,
   `forward/backward_heading_same_level`, `outline_up_heading`,
   `last_child_heading` are partly native and partly use stars or
   `item` and `headline`. The grep shows `files/heading.lua`, `fey/mappings.lua`
   and `utils/treesitter` as the main users.
-  Reuse:  the working ones.
-  Fey:  Lua audit. Go through each mapping once, write down which use fey
   node types already, port the rest (`toggle_heading` turns a list item or
   plain line into a heading with the next signature at the right level).
-  Triggers:  existing mappings.
-  Vault:  none.
-  After:  nothing.

  III.N. Retire FeyFiles

-  Now:  `Fey:init` still loads `FeyFiles` for `fey_agenda_files`, builds a
   `FeyFile` for every file and keeps `fey.buffers`. Several mappings use
   `self.files:get_current_file()`.
-  Reuse:  `FeyFile` for open buffers (reindexing, edits).
-  Fey:  Lua. Keep `FeyFile` as the editor side object for the current
   buffer. Drop the preload of all agenda files, load lazily, and make
   `files` a thin cache of open buffers. Update `Fey:init` and the auto
   instance keys.
-  Triggers:  autocmds in `Fey:setup_autocmds` stay.
-  Vault:  yes, replaces the preload.
-  After:  II.J.

  III.O. Export

-  Now:  `export/init.lua` shells out to pandoc and emacs for org input, with
   `fey_custom_exports`.
-  Reuse:  the job runner, the menu, the custom export hook.
-  Fey:  decide between a Lua exporter that walks the tree (markdown, html)
   and a pandoc reader. Large and independent. The databases already export
   (`db/export.lua`).
-  Triggers:  keybind `fey_export` `<prefix>e`.
-  Vault:  yes, for exporting a set of files or a whole label.
-  After:  everything above.

  III.P. Config, mappings, help and docs

-  Now:  `config/defaults.lua` still has org only options and many commented
   mappings; `objects/help.lua` and `show_help`; the docs `doc/fey.txt`,
   `docs/*.org` (the old org docs), `legal/` and the generated `doc/fey_api.txt`;
   `health.lua`.
-  Reuse:  the structure.
-  Fey:  go task by task: when a feature is finished, uncomment its mapping,
   add its option to `_meta.lua` with `vim.validate`, and add a section to
   `README.fey`. At the end remove options of features that will not be
   ported. The rewrite of `docs/*.org` and the regeneration of `doc/*.txt` are
   III.S, the renaming of old org words is III.R.
-  Triggers:  none.
-  Vault:  `:checkhealth fey` should report vault state, schema version, stale
   index and unknown tags.
-  After:  continuous.

Toggles (2026-10-06): there are enough toggles now to want a tree of their
own. Today `<prefix>Tc` hides or shows the task tags, and the older toggles
sit at `<prefix>!e`, `<prefix>?e` and the global config toggles. Proposal:
`<prefix>T` is the toggle root, with `Tc` for the concealment, and the others
get a letter under it (`Te` for run commands on enter, `Ts` for apply settings
on enter, `Tb` and `Tw` for the buffer and cwd config) while the old keys keep
working for a release. To decide.

  III.Q. Tests

-  Now:  `tests/api.lua`, `tests/query.lua`, `tests/db.lua` cover the vault,
   query and databases. There are no tests for heading, date, agenda or the
   grammar rules added here.
-  Fey:  for every grammar change add a corpus file in `tree-sitter-fey/test/corpus`
   (the checkbox, and any `metadata` rule). For every tag add a headless Lua
   test like `tests/api.lua`: parse, write, re-index, read back.
-  Triggers:  the pre-commit hook in `scripts/`.
-  Vault:  the fixture vault is the same temp directory pattern as in
   `tests/api.lua`.
-  After:  continuous.


  III.R. Terminology: org words become Fey words

-  Now:  the code, the options, the mappings, the events and the docs still
   speak orgmode where Fey differs, and some of the old words now mean
   something else in Fey. The worst one is "tag": in org it is a word of a
   headline (`:work:`), in Fey it is a piece of syntax, and the headline tags
   are labels. Code that says `get_tags` and `fey_tags_column` while meaning
   labels will confuse whoever maintains it later.
-  Reuse:  every name stays where it is until its area is finished, then it is
   renamed in one go. Old public names keep working for a release as
   deprecated aliases.
-  Fey:  Lua only, a mechanical rename plus a check that keeps it done.
   The map, to be extended while working through the code:

| org word                       | Fey word                      | where it shows                                           |
+================================+===============================+==========================================================+
| headline                       | heading                       | comments, docs, config text                              |
| tags of a headline, `:a:b:`    | labels                        | get_tags, set_tags, add_tag, align_tags, tags_to_string  |
| (the same, in options)         | labels                        | fey_tags_column, fey_use_tag_inheritance, fey_agenda_remove_tags |
| (the same, in mappings)        | labels                        | fey_set_tags_command, the agenda tags views and filters  |
| tag (the word, syntax)         | tag                           | stays, and now only means the syntax                     |
| stars                          | signature                     | fey_hide_leading_signature is already right, the rest not |
| headline text, item            | title                         | get_child_node item, FeyFile:get_title                   |
| TODO keyword                   | status (keyword and priority) | todo_state, fey_todo_keywords stay, text says status     |
| plan, planning line            | planning tags                 | get_plan_dates, plan, FeyPlanDateTypes                   |
| timestamp                      | date                          | fey_time_stamp, fey_timestamp_up, to_wrapped_string      |
| property drawer, drawer        | prop tag, pair tag            | get_drawer, add_to_drawer, fey_log_into_drawer           |
| directive, `#+name:`           | key of the document data      | get_directive, get_directive_properties                  |
| agenda files                   | the vault                     | fey_agenda_files, FeyFiles, fey_agenda_text_search_extra_files |
| src block, babel               | fenced block named src        | is_src_block, end_src in indentkeys                      |
| dynamic block                  | result tag                    | docs, capture, clock report                              |
| org file, `.org`               | Fey file, `.fey`              | docs/*.org, is_org_file style helpers                    |
| outline path                   | path of a heading             | get_outline_path (the index calls it `path`)             |
| directory with a `.fey` folder | hollow                        | fey.hollow.*, comments, commands, options                |
| the database in `.fey` (the index)| vault                         | fey.vault.*; a vault belongs to one hollow               |
| the master vault, the top      | court                         | fey_court_dir, court, the `court` keyword                |
| org, orgmode, nvim-orgmode     | fey                           | only in the credits and the history                      |

   Steps. 1. A script `scripts/terms.lua` lists every use of the old words by
   file, as the inventory and as the check. 2. Rename area by area: internal
   names outright, public names (config options, mapping names, user
   commands, events, the Lua API) get an alias with a deprecation notice, and
   the options get a migration table in `config:extend` that maps the old
   name to the new one and warns once. 3. A deny list in the pre-commit hook
   fails on a new use of an old word, outside the credits, the legal files,
   the changelog and this file. 4. After the rename read every remaining use of
   the word tag: it must mean the syntax. 5. Put the table above in
   `legal/contributing.fey` as the glossary for maintainers.
-  Triggers:  the pre-commit hook; `:checkhealth fey` reports old option names
   that are still in use.
-  Vault:  none. The index already says labels, container and heading path, and
   the renames bring the code in line with it.
-  After:  all of phase 1 and phase 2: rename once everything is ported, so
   finished code is not touched twice. New code follows the rule of I.C
   meanwhile.

  III.S. Documentation: the final pass

-  Now:  `docs/*.org` (index, tutorial, configuration, installation, plugins,
   troubleshoot, contributing, changelog) describe nvim-orgmode, `doc/fey.txt`
   and `doc/fey_api.txt` are generated from them and from the code, and
   `README.fey` covers only part of the plugin.
-  Reuse:  the generators (`scripts/gendoc.lua`, `scripts/panvimdoc.lua`,
   `scripts/build_docs.sh`, `scripts/generate_changelog.lua`), the structure of
   the old docs where it still fits, the sections written along the way (I.C,
   II.R).
-  Fey:  Fey markup, and the docs are Fey files, which also makes them a vault
   that can be queried. Rewrite `docs/*.org` as `.fey`: installation (grammar
   install, vault), a tutorial that follows the real features (headings and
   signatures, lists, tables, tags and the tag families of this list, dates and
   planning, status, labels, properties, the agenda, refile, capture, clock,
   queries and database views), the configuration reference generated from
   `config/_meta.lua` and `defaults.lua` (every option has a type and a
   default there), the troubleshoot page, and the contributing page with the
   glossary of III.R. Regenerate `doc/*.txt`, and the help tags. The
   changelog is generated from the commit history, and starts at the first Fey
   release, the org history is one paragraph in the credits.
-  Triggers:  `scripts/build_docs.sh`; the pre-commit hook rebuilds the help
   files when the sources change.
-  Vault:  yes. The docs folder is a vault: the index checks that every
   `link` and `section` tag in the docs resolves, and the options page can be a
   `query` over the config documentation instead of a hand written table.
-  After:  III.R and everything before it.

